===Verb===
{{en-verb|drives|driving|drove|past2=drave|past2_qual=archaic|past3=driv|past3_qual=dialectal|driven|past_ptc2=druv|past_ptc2_qual=dialectal|past_ptc3_qual=dialectal|past_ptc3=drove}}

# To [[operate]] a [[vehicle]]
## {{lb|en|transitive|ergative}} To [[operate]] (a [[wheeled]] [[motorize]]d [[vehicle]]).
##: {{ux|en|'''drive''' a car}}
##: {{ux|en|This SUV '''drives''' insanely smoothly—it's like it knows what I want before I do.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To travel by operating a wheeled motorized vehicle.
##: {{ux|en|I '''drive''' to work every day.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To convey (a person, etc.) in a wheeled motorized vehicle.
##: {{ux|en|My cousin '''drove''' me to the airport.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|slang|aviation}} To [[operate]] (an [[aircraft]]).
##: {{ux|en|'''drive''' a 737}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To direct a vehicle powered by a horse, ox or similar animal.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|II|6|text=There is a litter ready; lay him in’t<br>And '''drive''' towards Dover, friend, where thou shalt meet<br>Both welcome and protection.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=2|passage=We '''drove''' back to the office with some concern on my part at the prospect of so large a case. Sunning himself on the board steps, I saw for the first time Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke. He was dressed out in broad gaiters and bright tweeds, like an English tourist, and his face might have belonged to Dagon, idol of the Philistines.}}
# To [[compel]] to move
## {{senseid|en|compel onward}} {{lb|en|transitive}} {{q|especially of animals}} To [[impel]] or urge onward by force; to push forward; to [[compel]] to move on.
##: {{ux|en|to '''drive''' twenty thousand head of cattle from Texas to the Kansas railheads''; ''to '''drive''' sheep out of a field}}
## {{senseid|en|compel outward}} {{lb|en|transitive}} {{q|especially animals}} To cause to flee out of.
##: {{syn|en|flush|flush out|scare up}}
##: {{ux|en|The hunting  dog '''drove''' the birds out of the tall grass.}}
##: {{ux|en|We'll '''drive''' the enemy from these lands once and for all.}}
# To cause to move by the [[application]] of [[physical]] force
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[provide]] an [[impetus]] for [[motion]] or other [[physical]] [[change]], to [[move]] an [[object]] by means of the [[provision]] of [[force]] thereto.
##: {{ux|en|You '''drive''' nails into wood with a hammer.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cause]] (a [[mechanism]]) to [[operate]].
##: {{ux|en|The pistons '''drive''' the crankshaft.}}
## {{senseid|en|sports-verb}} {{lb|en|intransitive|sports|cricket|tennis|baseball}} To [[hit]] the [[ball]] with a [[drive#Noun|drive]].
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To separate the lighter (feathers or down) from the heavier, by exposing them to a current of air.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[displace]] either physically or non-physically, through the [[application]] of force.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|IV|7|text=One fire '''drives''' out one fire; one nail, one nail;<br>Rights by rights falter, strengths by strengths do fail.}}
# To compel to undergo a non-physical change
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[provide]] an [[impetus]] for a [[change]] in one's [[situation]] or [[state of mind]].
##: {{ux|en|My husband's constant harping about the condition of the house threatens to '''drive''' me to distraction.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[motivate]]; to [[provide]] an [[incentive]] for.
##: {{ux|en|What '''drives''' a person to run a marathon?}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[compel]], [[exert]] pressure, [[coerce]] {{q|to do something}}.
##: {{ux|en|Their debts finally '''drove''' them to sell the business.}}
##* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|chapter=19|page=186|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A12229.0001.001|text=He '''driuen''' to dismount, threatned, if I did not the like, to doo as much for my horse, as Fortune had done for his.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|V|4|text=But darkness and the gloomy shade of death<br>Environ you, till mischief and despair<br>'''Drive''' you to break your necks or hang yourselves!}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to become.
##: {{ux|en|This constant complaining is going to '''drive''' me insane.}}
##: {{ux|en|You are '''driving''' me crazy!}}
##* '''1855''', {{w|Alfred, Lord Tennyson}}, ''Maud'', XXV, 1. in ''{{w|Maud, and Other Poems}}'', London: Edward Moxon, p. 90,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/maudotherpoems00tennrich]</sup>
##*: And then to hear a dead man chatter
##*: Is enough to '''drive''' one mad.
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=4|passage=One morning I had been '''driven''' to the precarious refuge afforded by the steps of the inn, after rejecting offers from the Celebrity to join him in a variety of amusements. But even here I was not free from interruption, for he was seated on a horse-block below me, playing with a fox terrier.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[motivate]] through the [[application]] or [[demonstration]] of force; to [[coerce]], [[intimidate]]{{,}} or [[threaten]]; to [[impel]] or [[urge]] onward in such a way.
##: {{ux|en|Frothing at the mouth and threatening expulsion, Coach relentlessly '''drove''' the team to more laps of the pitch.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1881|translator=w:Benjamin Jowett|chapter=w:Thucydides|title=w:History of the Peloponnesian War|location=Oxford|publisher=Clarendon|section=Volume I, Book 4, p. 247|url=https://archive.org/details/a609583001thucuoft
|passage={{...}} Demosthenes desired them first to put in at Pylos and not to proceed on their voyage until they had done what he wanted. They objected, but it so happened that a storm came on and '''drove''' them into Pylos.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[urge]], [[press]], or [[bring]] to a [[point]] or [[state]].
##: {{ux|en|The negotiations were '''driven''' to completion minutes before the final deadline.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 January 12|author=Nigel Harris|title=Comment: Unhappy start to 2022|journal=RAIL|issue=948|page=3|text=And now we're waiting for the very same people to establish GBR, '''drive''' through urgently needed fares reform, and come up with imaginative and effective train operating contracts...}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[move]] [[forcefully]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|II|2|text={{...}} Unequal match’d,<br>Pyrrhus at Priam '''drives''', in rage strikes wide;}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|book=1|line=146-148|text=Thus while the Pious Prince his Fate bewails,<br>Fierce ''[[Boreas]]'' '''drove''' against his flying Sails.<br>And rent the Sheets {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1833|author=w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|chapter=w:The Lotos-Eaters|title=Poems|location=London|publisher=Edward Moxon|page=113|url=https://archive.org/details/poemstennalfr00tennrich
|passage=Time '''driveth''' onward fast,<br>And in a little while our lips are dumb.}}
#* {{RQ:Prescott Philip 2|volume=I|chapter=1|page=7|text=Charles, ill in body and mind, and glad to escape from his enemies under cover of the night and a '''driving''' tempest, was at length compelled to sign the treaty of Passau {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:H.G. Wells|chapter=The "Thunder Child."|title=w:The War of the Worlds|url=https://books.google.com/books/about/The_War_of_the_Worlds.html?id=bQlHAAAAYAAJ&printsec=frontcover&source=kp_read_button&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0#v=onepage&q&f=false|accessdate=24 November 2022|location=Leipzig|publisher={{w|Tauchnitz publishers|Bernhard Tauchnitz}}|year=1898|page=175|text=It would seem they were regarding this new antagonist with astonishment.  To their intelligence, it may be, the giant was even such another as themselves.  The ''Thunder Child'' fired no gun, but simply '''drove''' full speed towards them.  It was probably her not firing that enabled her to get so near the enemy as she did.  They did not know what to make of her.  One shell, and they would have sent her to the bottom forthwith with the Heat-Ray.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 29, 2010|author=Mark Vesty|work=BBC
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9322845.stm Wigan 2-2 Arsenal]
|passage=The impressive Frenchman '''drove''' forward with purpose down the right before cutting infield and darting in between Vassiriki Diaby and Koscielny.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[move]]d or [[propel]]led [[forcefully]] (''especially of a ship'').
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Pericles|III|Prologue|text={{...}} as a duck for life that dives,<br>So up and down the poor ship '''drives''':}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1743|author={{w|Robert Drury (sailor)|Robert Drury}}|title=The Pleasant, and Surprizing Adventures of Mr. Robert Drury, during his Fifteen Years Captivity on the Island of Madagascar|url=https://archive.org/details/pleasantandsurp00drurgoog|page=12|location=London
|passage={{...}} the Captain {{...}} order’d the Cable to be cut, and let the Ship '''drive''' nearer the Land, where she soon beat to pieces:}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To carry or to keep [[in motion]]; to [[conduct]]; to [[prosecute]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1694|author=w:Jeremy Collier|title=Miscellanies in Five Essays|location=London|publisher=Sam. Keeble & Jo. Hindmarsh|chapter=Of General Kindness|page=69|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A33912.0001.001
|passage=You know the Trade of Life can’t be '''driven''' without ''Partners;'' there is a reciprocal Dependance between the ''Greatest'' and the ''Least''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To clear, by forcing away what is contained.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|book=1|line=744-745|text=We come not with design of wastful Prey,<br>To '''drive''' the Country, force the Swains away:}}
# {{lb|en|mining}} To dig horizontally; to cut a horizontal gallery or tunnel.
#* '''1852-1866''', {{w|Charles Tomlinson (scientist)|Charles Tomlinson}}, ''Cyclopaedia of Useful Arts and Manufactures''
#*: If the miners find no ore, they '''drive''' or cut a gallery from the pit a short distance at right angles to the direction of the lodes found
# {{lb|en|American football}} To put together a [[drive]] (''n.''): to string together offensive plays and advance the ball down the field.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[distrain]] for [[rent]].
# To be the [[dominant]] party in a [[sex act]]. {{rfex|en}}

